### Provider-free deterministic V5 smoke — pass

The final smoke patched the universe helper to a fixed local list and used synthetic OHLCV and an
in-process policy client. It exercised the real `PortfolioSimulator.run()` loop, checkpoint write,
completed resume, gap path, friction, pending serialization, partial-pair rejection, and legacy
close accounting. Scratch files were removed afterward.

```json
{"checkpoint_resume_exact": true, "friction_usd": 15.0, "gap_priority": "stop_loss", "legacy_cash": 1010.0, "legacy_schema": 4, "next_open": ["2025-10-03", "2025-10-06"], "pending_round_trip": true}
```

Assertions included:

- close-derived `policy_exit` stayed open on 2025-10-03 and sold at 2025-10-06 open;
- a 90.0 opening gap below the stop sold with `stop_loss`, not the queued policy exit;
- BUY and SELL fill logs and positive friction totals were present;
- live and completed-resume transaction rows, fill rows, cost totals, normalized date keys, and
  equity values were identical;
- a V5 pending exit round-tripped exactly;
- a legacy 900.0 cash account selling one share at 110.0 ended at exactly 1010.0, emitted no V5
  fill rows, and retained checkpoint schema 4; and
- a partial execution-profile/friction pair raised `ValueError`.

An earlier version of the end-to-end smoke accidentally allowed the existing universe helper to
attempt its provider fallback; the sandbox blocked both requests before data was returned. The
final evidence run was explicitly provider-free.

